title: '[pallet-assets-precompiles] `approve`/`permit` saturate on `U256 > Balance::MAX`'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: "## Summary\n\n`approve` and `permit` now saturate the *stored* allowance\
    \ at `Balance::MAX` when `call.value > Balance::MAX`, instead of reverting at the\
    \ `U256 → Balance` conversion. MetaMask, Uniswap, and most DEX routers hard-code\
    \ `type(uint256).max` in approval calls — the universal \"infinite allowance\"\
    \ idiom — and the previous behaviour broke every one of those flows on chains\
    \ where the runtime `Balance` (u128 or u64) can't hold `U256::MAX`.\n\nSaturating\
    \ the stored allowance is lossless: total supply is also capped at `Balance::MAX`,\
    \ so a larger allowance grants no additional spending power. The emitted `Approval`\
    \ event still carries the raw `call.value`, matching the ERC-20 / OpenZeppelin\
    \ convention so EVM wallets and indexers (subgraphs, MetaMask token-activity views,\
    \ explorers) keep working without precompile-specific knowledge — including\
    \ the `value == uint256.max` \"Unlimited approval\" sentinel pattern. Consumers\
    \ needing the exact stored allowance can call `allowance()`.\n\n## Sentinel semantics\n\
    \nOnly the literal `call.value == U256::MAX` idiom is treated as an \"infinite\
    \ allowance\" sentinel. The `(verifying_contract, owner, spender)` triple is flagged\
    \ in `permit::InfiniteApprovals` and `transferFrom` will skip the approval decrement\
    \ for the flagged triple — matching OpenZeppelin's `_spendAllowance` rule\
    \ on Ethereum mainnet, where `type(uint256).max` (not `Balance::MAX`) is the sentinel.\
    \ Any subsequent finite write to the same triple clears the flag, so a direct\
    \ `approve(spender, Balance::MAX)` (a finite value, not the sentinel idiom) decrements\
    \ normally.\n\n## Destruction cleanup\n\nA new `pallet_assets::AssetsCallback` adapter\
    \ pair (`ClearLocalInfiniteApprovals<PREFIX, T>` and `ClearForeignInfiniteApprovals<PREFIX,\
    \ T>`) wipes `permit::InfiniteApprovals` rows on asset destruction. Without this,\
    \ destroying and re-creating an inline-id (local) asset would reuse the same precompile\
    \ address and inherit the previous incarnation's sentinel flags, making `allowance()`\
    \ return `U256::MAX` for a freshly-created asset with no approval row. Runtimes\
    \ must wire these adapters into `pallet_assets::Config::CallbackHandle` for each\
    \ instance they expose via this precompile; `ClearForeignInfiniteApprovals` must\
    \ precede `ForeignAssetId` in the callback tuple (the index lookup depends on\
    \ the asset_id→index mapping still being in place).\n\n## Versioning\n\nBumped\
    \ as **patch**: every change is a strict superset of the previous behaviour —\
    \ `approve`/`permit` calls that previously reverted with `U256 > Balance::MAX`\
    \ now succeed, no previously-successful call changes outcome, and the destruction\
    \ cleanup is a no-op for runtimes that don't wire the new callback adapters. The\
    \ Rust crate API gains two public types (`ClearLocalInfiniteApprovals`, `ClearForeignInfiniteApprovals`)\
    \ and the `precompile_address` helper; no existing items are renamed or removed.\n\
    \n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `cargo test -p pallet-assets-precompiles` passes"
crates:
- name: pallet-assets-precompiles
  bump: patch
